what is ci-cd

ci = continuous integration
cd = continuous delivery / continuous deployment

ci-cd is a devops practice where code is automatically built, tested, and deployed whenever changes are made.

simple flow:

code → build → test → deploy

goal:

faster delivery

fewer bugs

less manual work

why ci-cd is needed

before ci-cd:

manual testing

manual deployment

high chances of errors

slow releases

after ci-cd:

everything automated

fast and reliable releases

easy to detect bugs early

continuous integration (ci)

continuous integration means:
developers frequently push code to a shared repository and every change is automatically tested.

process:

developer writes code

pushes to git

pipeline runs automatically

build + test happens

benefits:

early bug detection

better code quality

no integration issues

continuous delivery (cd)

continuous delivery means:
after successful testing, code is ready to be deployed automatically.

deployment may still require manual approval.

continuous deployment

continuous deployment means:
code is automatically deployed to production without manual approval.

difference:

delivery → manual approval before deploy

deployment → fully automatic

stages in ci-cd pipeline

a pipeline is divided into stages:

source stage

code stored in git (github, gitlab)

build stage

install dependencies

compile code

test stage

run unit tests

run integration tests

deploy stage

deploy app to server or cloud

pipeline workflow (end-to-end)

developer → git push →
ci pipeline starts →
build →
test →
cd pipeline →
deploy →
application live

tools used in ci-cd

popular tools:

Jenkins

GitHub Actions

GitLab CI/CD

container and deployment:

docker

kubernetes

infrastructure:

Terraform

how terraform fits in ci-cd

terraform is used to create infrastructure before deployment.

flow:

pipeline runs

terraform creates servers (ec2, vpc, etc.)

application is deployed on created infrastructure

important concepts

pipeline

a sequence of automated steps

stage

a group of tasks (build, test, deploy)

job

a single task inside a stage

trigger

event that starts pipeline (push, pull request)

artifact

output of build (jar file, docker image)

example of simple pipeline

steps:

push code to github

pipeline triggers

build docker image

run tests

deploy to server

